# UTF-8 Encoding Rules on Windows

This project uses UTF-8 for all source files, scripts, docs, configs, and generated text files.

When working on Windows or PowerShell:

- Never write project files using PowerShell redirection operators `>` or `>>`.
- Never use `Out-File`, `Set-Content`, `Add-Content`, or `Export-Csv` without an explicit UTF-8 encoding.
- Do not use Windows PowerShell 5.1 terminal commands to create or overwrite source files when editor/patch editing is available.
- Prefer editor/patch-based file edits over shell-based file generation.
- If shell-based writing is necessary, use explicit UTF-8 encoding and verify the written file is not UTF-16.
- If a file already exists, preserve its encoding unless the project explicitly requires converting it to UTF-8.
- If generated text contains garbled characters, stop and inspect the file encoding before rewriting it.

Preferred PowerShell write pattern for generated text:

```powershell
$utf8NoBom = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false)
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $content, $utf8NoBom)
[System.IO.File]::AppendAllText($path, $content, $utf8NoBom)
```

Acceptable PowerShell patterns:

```powershell
Set-Content -Path .\file.txt -Value $content -Encoding utf8
Add-Content -Path .\file.txt -Value $content -Encoding utf8
$content | Out-File -FilePath .\file.txt -Encoding utf8
Export-Csv -Path .\data.csv -Encoding utf8 -NoTypeInformation
```

Avoid these patterns:

```powershell
"content" > file.txt
"content" >> file.txt
"content" | Out-File file.txt
Set-Content file.txt "content"
Add-Content file.txt "content"
Export-Csv file.csv
```

After generating or overwriting files, check for UTF-16 BOMs when the file may contain non-ASCII text:

```powershell
$bytes = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($path)
if ($bytes.Length -ge 2 -and (($bytes[0] -eq 0xFF -and $bytes[1] -eq 0xFE) -or ($bytes[0] -eq 0xFE -and $bytes[1] -eq 0xFF))) {
  throw "File was written as UTF-16, rewrite it as UTF-8: $path"
}
```

Before running scripts that may read or write non-ASCII text in PowerShell, normalize the session:

```powershell
chcp 65001 | Out-Null
[Console]::InputEncoding = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new()
[Console]::OutputEncoding = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new()
$OutputEncoding = [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new()
$PSDefaultParameterValues['Out-File:Encoding'] = 'utf8'
$PSDefaultParameterValues['Set-Content:Encoding'] = 'utf8'
$PSDefaultParameterValues['Add-Content:Encoding'] = 'utf8'
$PSDefaultParameterValues['Export-Csv:Encoding'] = 'utf8'
```

If you are about to create or overwrite a file from a terminal command, first explain why editor/patch editing is not enough, then use an explicit UTF-8 write method.
